talk(476,"1979-11-19","A. A. Beilinson","P000885-P000886","Dilogarithms, tame symbols and regulators of curves",
     r"Beilinson explains the real dilogarithmic regulator, its Steinberg identity and tame-symbol periods, then refines it to a complex multiplicative regulator. The latter matches his short 1980 publication closely. The opening remark about zeta functions has no sufficiently precise arithmetic conjecture. Source errors include the sign of the zeta(2) integral, a missing period normalization, and calling a multiplicative skew symbol symmetric. The distinction between K-theory of the curve and its function field is essential.")
item(476,"P000885","theorem","The zeta(2) integral has a missing minus sign",
     r"Equation (3) states \(\int_0^1\log x\,dx/(1-x)=\sum 1/n^2\), with no visible minus sign in the scan.",
     r"The correct identity is \(\int_0^1\log x\,dx/(1-x)=-\sum_{n\ge1}n^{-2}=-\pi^2/6\). Expanding \((1-x)^{-1}\) and applying monotone convergence to the nonnegative function \(-\log x/(1-x)\) justifies termwise integration.",
     r"This is a classical dilogarithm evaluation; compare \citet[Chapter I, \S1]{zagier2007dilogarithm}. Its sign is also forced by the negative integrand on \((0,1)\).",
     ["zagier2007dilogarithm"],literal="false as written",match="partial",reconstruction=r"The lower limit on the sum and the minus sign are supplied; the elementary justification is editorial.",status=r"the corrected identity is settled; both transcriptions faithfully retain the notebook's sign error.")
item(476,"P000885","remark","Bloch's proposed replacement of zeta(2) by a curve zeta function",
     r"The introduction says generalization to curves, names Bloch, and replaces the right-hand zeta value by the curve's zeta function.",
     r"No base number field, arithmetic model, value of the complex parameter, order of vanishing, regulator determinant or rational factor is recorded. There is therefore no uniquely recoverable special-value formula or conjecture.",
     r"\citet[\S1 and bibliography item 1]{beilinson1980regulators} explicitly relates the real regulator to Bloch's 1978 Irvine lectures. Its later \S2 concerns higher regulators and L-values, but that is not evidence that all those conjectural quantifiers were stated here.",
     ["beilinson1980regulators"],cls="F",literal="not determinable",match="thematic only",status=r"the historical regulator context is identifiable; the vague zeta remark remains unsuitable for an independent problem record.",confidence="medium")
item(476,"P000885","construction","A closed real regulator one-form",
     r"For nowhere-zero holomorphic functions on a Riemann surface the notes define \(\omega_{f,g}=\log|f|d\arg g-\log|g|d\arg f\) and infer that integration along paths is well defined.",
     r"On a Riemann surface where \(f,g\) are holomorphic units, \(d\arg f=\operatorname{Im}(df/f)\) is globally defined and \(d\omega_{f,g}=0\). Hence its integrals depend only on the relative homology class of a path with fixed endpoints; closedness does not assert independence of every path between those endpoints.",
     r"The real regulator is attributed to Bloch's 1978 lectures in \citet[\S1, paragraph following Lemma 1]{beilinson1980regulators}. The displayed differential identity also follows directly from the Cauchy--Riemann equations.",
     ["beilinson1980regulators"],reconstruction=r"The global one-form is distinguished from a globally chosen argument function; path homotopy or homology is the required qualification.",match="partial",status=r"the closed-form construction is established; nonzero periods are precisely part of its regulator content.")
item(476,"P000885","theorem","Bilinearity and alternation of the real regulator form",
     r"Equations (6)--(7) give additivity in a product of functions and antisymmetry under interchanging them.",
     r"For holomorphic units, \(\omega_{f_1f_2,g}=\omega_{f_1,g}+\omega_{f_2,g}\), \(\omega_{f,g_1g_2}=\omega_{f,g_1}+\omega_{f,g_2}\), and \(\omega_{g,f}=-\omega_{f,g}\). These are equalities of differential forms, not merely of cohomology classes.",
     r"These elementary identities underlie Bloch's real regulator, recalled by \citet[\S1]{beilinson1980regulators}; they are already implicit in the logarithmic formula.",
     ["beilinson1980regulators"],literal="correct",status=r"the identities are exact on the common domain of invertibility.")
item(476,"P000885","theorem","The single-valued dilogarithm proves the Steinberg relation",
     r"The form \(\omega_{f,1-f}\) is asserted to be exact. Equation (9) writes its primitive using a logarithm term and the imaginary part of an integral, and emphasizes single-valuedness.",
     r"The Bloch--Wigner dilogarithm \(D(z)=\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Li}_2(z)+\log|z|\arg(1-z)\) is single valued on \(\mathbb C\setminus\{0,1\}\), extends continuously by zero at \(0,1,\infty\), and satisfies \(dD(z)=\log|z|d\arg(1-z)-\log|1-z|d\arg z\). Consequently \(\omega_{f,1-f}=d(D\circ f)\) whenever \(f,1-f\) are holomorphic units.",
     r"\citet[Chapter I, \S3]{zagier2007dilogarithm} records the precise single-valued function and differential. The pre-1979 Bloch regulator context is confirmed in \citet[\S1]{beilinson1980regulators}.",
     ["zagier2007dilogarithm","beilinson1980regulators"],reconstruction=r"Individual logarithms and the complex dilogarithm require local branches; their stated combination is the global function. The lower endpoint zero is understood by its continuous limit.",status=r"this exactness identity and hence the real Steinberg relation are established.")
item(476,"P000885","remark","David Wigner and continuous cohomology of SL(2)",
     r"The notebook attributes the single-valued function to D. Wigner and says he connected it to continuous cohomology of \(SL(2)\).",
     r"Fix \(x\in\mathbb P^1(\mathbb C)\). On generic quadruples set \(c_x(g_0,g_1,g_2,g_3)=D(\operatorname{cr}(g_0x,g_1x,g_2x,g_3x))\). The five-term identity makes this a homogeneous measurable real three-cocycle for \(GL_2(\mathbb C)\), restricting to \(SL_2(\mathbb C)\); it represents the nonzero degree-three volume class under the measurable/continuous cohomology comparison. Changing \(x\) gives a cohomologous cocycle.",
     r"\citet[\S3, p.~117, eqs.~(10)--(12)]{goncharov1993classical} explicitly attributes this construction to Wigner and places it in the 1970s. The 1993 chapter is the checked exposition, not the first-circulation date of Wigner's observation; no original Wigner preprint date was verified.",
     ["goncharov1993classical"],literal="correct with missing hypotheses",match="partial",reconstruction=r"The complex group, real coefficients, degree, generic quadruples and comparison map are supplied from the literature. This identifies a standard precise version of the note; the notebook itself suppresses these data.",status=r"the volume-cocycle interpretation is established; the source's omission of field and degree is recorded rather than treated as an open problem.",confidence="medium")
item(476,"P000885","theorem","A puncture period and the tame symbol",
     r"Equation (10) equates a small-loop integral of \(\omega_{f,g}\) with \(\log|f^{v_x(g)}/g^{v_x(f)}|(x)\), without a factor \(2\pi\).",
     r"Let \(f=z^m a(z)\), \(g=z^n b(z)\) near \(x\), with \(a(0)b(0)\ne0\). For a positively oriented small circle \(\gamma_x\), \(\int_{\gamma_x}\omega_{f,g}=2\pi\log|a(0)^n/b(0)^m|=2\pi\log|\partial_x\{f,g\}|\), where \(\partial_x\{f,g\}=(-1)^{mn}a(0)^n/b(0)^m\).",
     r"The complex local tame-symbol identity is \citet[\S1, Lemma 1(b)]{beilinson1980regulators}; taking logarithmic absolute values gives the corrected real normalization.",
     ["beilinson1980regulators"],reconstruction=r"Orientation, local expansions and the tame sign are specified. The tame sign disappears under absolute value but not in the later complex symbol.",literal="false as written",match="partial",status=r"the corrected period identity is exact. The missing factor is a faithful notebook error, not a translation issue.")
item(476,"P000885-P000886","definition","Symbols present K2 of the function field, not automatically of the curve",
     r"The notes quotient symbols by bimultiplicativity, antisymmetry and the Steinberg relation, then call the result both \(K_2(X)\) and K2 of its rational function field.",
     r"For a field \(F\), Matsumoto's theorem identifies Quillen \(K_2(F)\) with the abelian group on \(\{f,g\}\), \(f,g\in F^\times\), subject to bimultiplicativity and \(\{f,1-f\}=0\) for \(f\ne0,1\). Skew symmetry follows from these relations. Taking \(F=\mathbb C(X)\) defines the function-field group; it does not by itself define \(K_2(X)\).",
     r"The result dates to Matsumoto's 1969 theorem, stated precisely in \citet[III.6.1]{weibel2013kbook}. The notebook's Milnor/Steinberg attribution is compatible with this older framework.",
     ["weibel2013kbook"],reconstruction=r"The ambient free group is made abelian, and the curve and function-field notation are separated.",literal="false as written",match="partial",status=r"the field presentation is established; the missing passage to the curve is supplied by localization in the following item.")
item(476,"P000885","theorem","The unique smooth projective completion of an algebraic curve",
     r"A parenthesis says an algebraic curve is compactified uniquely, for example by embedding it in projective space and taking the closure.",
     r"A smooth connected algebraic curve over \(\mathbb C\) has a smooth projective completion unique up to the unique isomorphism extending the given open curve. The normalization of a projective closure gives that smooth completion. A raw projective closure can be singular; arbitrary open Riemann surfaces need not be algebraic or of finite type.",
     r"This is the classical smooth-curve model used in the regulator construction of \citet[\S1]{beilinson1980regulators}; it is not a new theorem announced by this talk.",
     ["beilinson1980regulators"],reconstruction=r"Smoothness, algebraicity, normalization and uniqueness relative to the given open immersion are the missing hypotheses. The preceding general Riemann surface is not silently assumed algebraic.",match="partial",status=r"the corrected completion statement is standard; the unrestricted topological or analytic reading is false.")
item(476,"P000886","construction","Localization and extension of the real regulator over punctures",
     r"Equation (12) writes \(K_2(X)\to K_2(\mathbb C(X))\to\bigoplus_x\mathbb C^*\). A sum of symbols with trivial local terms then gives a class in \(H^1(X,\mathbb R)\).",
     r"For a smooth algebraic curve, localization identifies the image of \(K_2(X)\to K_2(\mathbb C(X))\) with the kernel of the sum of tame symbols. If \(\xi=\sum_i n_i\{f_i,g_i\}\) has trivial tame symbol at every point of \(X\), the closed form \(\sum_i n_i\omega_{f_i,g_i}\), initially off its divisors, has zero small-loop periods and determines a unique class in \(H^1(X,\mathbb R)\). This defines the real regulator on \(K_2(X)\) by composition.",
     r"\citet[\S1, immediately after Lemma 1]{beilinson1980regulators} explicitly uses Quillen's localization theorem and identifies the resulting logarithmic absolute value with Bloch's regulator, already circulated in his 1978 lectures.",
     ["beilinson1980regulators"],reconstruction=r"The statement concerns an image/kernel exactness assertion. The notes do not supply a proof of injectivity of the first arrow, and no such assertion is needed for the regulator construction. Uniqueness is uniqueness of the extended cohomology class, not of an arbitrary differential form.",status=r"the regular-curve regulator construction is established; it makes no special-value conjecture without further arithmetic data.")
item(476,"P000886","construction","Beilinson's complex multiplicative regulator",
     r"Equation (16) defines an exponentiated logarithmic integral in cases according to the winding number of \(g\), with a sign \((-1)^{v_\gamma(f)v_\gamma(g)}\). The symbol is said to be bimultiplicative, symmetric and Steinberg, and to recover the real construction.",
     r"For a loop \(\gamma\) based at \(a\), avoid the divisors and continue chosen logarithms along the loop. A convenient equivalent formula is \[r_\gamma(f,g)=\exp\!\left[\frac{1}{2\pi i}\left(\int_\gamma\log f\,d\log g-\log g(a)\int_\gamma d\log f\right)\right].\] It is independent of the initial logarithm choices, homotopy invariant, bimultiplicative, multiplicatively skew, and satisfies \(r_\gamma(f,1-f)=1\). On a puncture circle it is the tame symbol. Moreover \(\log|r_\gamma(f,g)|=(2\pi)^{-1}\int_\gamma\omega_{f,g}\), so unramified symbols yield the complex refinement of the real regulator.",
     r"This is an exact construction match to \citet[\S1, eq.~(1) and Lemma 1]{beilinson1980regulators}, published in 1980. The journal records receipt on 21 December 1979; no earlier public preprint date was verified. The notebook documents the seminar on 19 November 1979. The paper also acknowledges an independent unpublished construction by Deligne.",
     ["beilinson1980regulators"],cls="A",reconstruction=r"The basepoint formula makes branch normalization explicit and avoids the notebook's case division. Multiplicative skew symmetry means \(r_\gamma(g,f)=r_\gamma(f,g)^{-1}\); literal symmetry would generally be false. Taking logarithmic absolute value, with the stated factor, is the recovery of the real regulator.",literal="false as written",match="exact",status=r"this regulator refinement was published soon after the seminar and is established. It is not merely a retrospective thematic link to later Beilinson conjectures.")
item(476,"P000886","theorem","Stable Steinberg extension and second group homology",
     r"For an affine curve the notes write \(K_2(X)=H_2(SL_n(A),\mathbb Z)\) and \(1\to K_2(X)\to St^X\to SL^X\to1\), where \(A\) is its ring of algebraic functions.",
     r"For a unital ring \(A\), the stable Steinberg group gives the universal central extension \(1\to K_2(A)\to\operatorname{St}(A)\to E(A)\to1\), and \(K_2(A)\cong H_2(E(A),\mathbb Z)\). If \(X=\operatorname{Spec}A\), its algebraic K2 is \(K_2(A)\). Replacing the stable elementary group by a particular \(SL_n(A)\) requires both the relevant equality with the elementary group and homology/K2 stability hypotheses.",
     r"The Kervaire--Steinberg theorem is stated in \citet[III.5.5; stability discussion III.5.5.2]{weibel2013kbook}, which traces the older Milnor/Steinberg theory. It does not assert the displayed equality for arbitrary \(n\).",
     ["weibel2013kbook"],reconstruction=r"The stable rank and elementary-group qualifications are essential. They are supplied rather than choosing an unjustified finite rank for the source.",literal="correct with missing hypotheses",match="partial",status=r"the stable statement is established; the source's unspecified finite-rank formulation is not a complete theorem.")
correction("P000885","equation (3)",r"Integral log x/(1-x) = sum 1/n^2",r"Integral log x/(1-x) = -sum_{n>=1} 1/n^2 = -pi^2/6","The original PNG has no minus sign, and the integrand is negative throughout (0,1).")
correction("P000885","equation (10)",r"Integral around puncture = log |f^v(g)/g^v(f)|",r"For ordinary positive winding, integral = 2 pi log |f^v(g)/g^v(f)|","Original PNG has no coefficient. Substitution f=z^m a, g=z^n b gives the factor 2pi; neither the preceding darg nor the loop integral is normalized in the notes.")
correction("P000885-P000886","K2 notation","The symbol group is K2(X) of the rational function field","Write K2(C(X)) for the field group and distinguish K2(X), whose image is characterized by tame-symbol localization.","Both source languages switch between curve and function-field notation. Equation (12) itself distinguishes them.",typ="ambiguity")
correction("P000886","symbol symmetry","symm. / симм.","Multiplicatively skew-symmetric: r(g,f)=r(f,g)^(-1)","Scan P000886 confirms симм.; Beilinson1980 section1 Lemma1 and the exponentiated formula give the inverse upon interchange.")
