talk(478,"1979-11-19","I. A. Batalin","P000887-P000888","Hamiltonian BRST quantization in a relativistic gauge",
     r"The second Batalin contribution on 19 November extends phase space by multiplier momenta and ghosts, introduces an odd nilpotent BRST charge and a gauge fermion, and sketches gauge independence by a field-dependent BRST change of variables. This is the contemporaneous Hamiltonian BFV framework, whose even Poisson bracket must be distinguished from the later BV antibracket. The statement about termination of supergravity ghost expansions remains model dependent.")
item(478,"P000887","construction","Promoting Lagrange multipliers to canonical variables",
     r"The multiplier \(\lambda\) is paired with a momentum \(\pi\), and the extended constraints are \(G_a=(\pi_\mu,T_\mu)\). Gauge conditions include \(-\dot\lambda^\mu+\chi^\mu\). The count is \((n+m)-2m=n-m\).",
     r"For a regular irreducible bosonic system with \(n\) canonical pairs and \(m\) independent first-class constraints, add \(m\) multiplier pairs. The extended phase space has dimension \(2(n+m)\), and its \(2m\) first-class constraints, when regularly reduced, leave dimension \(2(n-m)\). Differential gauge conditions for the multipliers can be encoded by a suitable extended Hamiltonian. This is a local degree-of-freedom count, not a proof that arbitrary gauges are admissible.",
     r"The contemporaneous publication leads are \citet[pp.~309--312]{batalinvilkovisky1977relativistic} and \citet[pp.~343--348]{fradkinfradkina1978quantization}. The later Hamiltonian framework is explicitly described in \citet[introduction and \S~II]{bering1997geometry}.",
     ["batalinvilkovisky1977relativistic","fradkinfradkina1978quantization","bering1997geometry"],reconstruction=r"The count refers to independent regular bosonic constraints. The overwritten sign and momentum index in source equation (4) are not silently repaired; the clear equations (6)--(7) determine the construction.",match="partial",verification="indirect",status=r"the regular extended-phase-space construction is established; the literal ambiguous formula (4) remains a transcription uncertainty.",confidence="medium")
item(478,"P000887","construction","Ghost canonical pairs and the BFV phase-space action",
     r"Equation (8) integrates \((q^A,p_A,\eta^a,\mathcal P_a)\) with phase \(\int(p_A\dot q^A+\mathcal P_a\dot\eta^a-H_{\rm complete})dt\).",
     r"For each constraint \(G_a\), introduce a ghost \(\eta^a\) of opposite parity and its conjugate momentum \(\mathcal P_a\), with ghost numbers \(+1\) and \(-1\). The full symplectic structure is even. With a chosen time-slicing, boundary states and integration prescription the displayed phase-space expression is the formal BFV partition functional.",
     r"The boson/fermion extension belongs to the 1977/1978 Hamiltonian publications just cited. \citet[\S~II and \S~V.2, eq.~(V.8)]{bering1997geometry} writes the later precise geometric version and states its assumptions.",
     ["batalinvilkovisky1977relativistic","fradkinfradkina1978quantization","bering1997geometry"],reconstruction=r"Ghost number, parity and boundary prescriptions are supplied. Odd ghost variables do not make the ambient Poisson bracket odd, and no BV antifields have been introduced.",match="partial",status=r"this is the standard Hamiltonian ghost extension; the displayed continuum integral remains formal without further analytic construction.")
item(478,"P000887-P000888","construction","Nilpotent Hamiltonian charge, invariant Hamiltonian and gauge fermion",
     r"Equations (9)--(11) require \(\{\Omega,\Omega\}=0\), \(\{H_1,\Omega\}=0\), and \(H_{\rm complete}=H_1-\{\Psi,\Omega\}\), with \(\Psi=\mathcal P_a\chi^a\).",
     r"Let the odd charge \(\Omega\) have ghost number one and satisfy the even-bracket master equation \(\{\Omega,\Omega\}=0\). Let \(H_1\) be even, ghost number zero, and BRST invariant. For any admissible odd gauge fermion \(\Psi\) of ghost number minus one, the displayed gauge-fixed Hamiltonian is again BRST invariant by the graded Jacobi identity.",
     r"\citet[\S~II, eq.~(II.6), and \S~V.2]{bering1997geometry} gives this Hamiltonian structure and attributes it to the earlier BFV work. The original 1977/1978 articles are indirect publication checks, not a claim that the 1979 notes anticipated the later Lagrangian BV formalism.",
     ["bering1997geometry","batalinvilkovisky1977relativistic","fradkinfradkina1978quantization"],reconstruction=r"Parities and ghost numbers make the equations precise; the sign of the gauge-fixing term is conventional once \(\Psi\) is fixed.",status=r"the algebraic BRST-invariance statement is exact; admissibility and quantum gauge independence require the additional hypotheses in item 1979-478.7.")
item(478,"P000888","claim","Solving the Hamiltonian BRST equations as a formal series",
     r"The speaker says the equations for \(\Omega\) and \(H_1\) can be solved as series in ghosts and ghost momenta whenever the constraints are first class.",
     r"For a regular irreducible first-class constraint system, with the usual acyclic constraint resolution locally, one can recursively construct a formal charge beginning \(\Omega=\eta^aG_a+\cdots\) and a BRST extension of a Hamiltonian preserving the constraints. Reducible systems need further ghosts; singular constraints need additional hypotheses. Formal solvability asserts neither convergence nor finite termination.",
     r"\citet[pp.~343--348]{fradkinfradkina1978quantization} is the contemporaneous graded-constraint publication lead. \citet[introduction]{bering1997geometry} confirms the later BFV framework but assumes, rather than proves, the existence of the charge used in that article. The original recursive proof was not recovered here.",
     ["fradkinfradkina1978quantization","bering1997geometry"],reconstruction=r"Regularity, irreducibility or a suitable resolution, locality and formality are necessary additions to the notebook's all-first-class wording.",match="partial",verification="indirect",status=r"the regular formal existence framework is established; an unrestricted singular-system theorem is not verified by the notes or the checked publication text.",confidence="medium")
item(478,"P000888","remark","Termination of the supergravity ghost expansion",
     r"The series are said usually to terminate in supergravity.",
     r"A finite ghost-degree bound depends on the particular supergravity theory, formulation, auxiliary fields and constraint generators. None is specified, so the word usually does not define a universal termination theorem.",
     r"The contemporary modified-rule lead is \citet[pp.~141--152]{kallosh1978modified}. The later open-algebra discussion in \citet[\S2.2]{fuster2005brst} confirms the relevant phenomenon but supplies no model-independent bound matching this phrase.",
     ["kallosh1978modified","fuster2005brst"],cls="F",literal="not determinable",match="thematic only",verification="indirect",status=r"model-specific constructions exist; this notebook remark remains too imprecise for a separate open-problem identifier.",confidence="medium")
item(478,"P000888","theorem","Hamiltonian BRST transformations and nilpotence",
     r"Equation (12) writes \(\delta X=\{X,\Omega\}\mu\), with \(\mu\) an odd constant.",
     r"The differential \(sX=\{X,\Omega\}\), with a consistent right/left derivation convention, has odd parity and satisfies \(s^2=0\) when \(\{\Omega,\Omega\}=0\). A constant odd parameter gives an even infinitesimal change of integration variables. The symplectic potential changes by an endpoint term, so invariance of an action also depends on boundary conditions.",
     r"\citet[\S~II, eq.~(II.6), and \S~V.2, eq.~(V.9)]{bering1997geometry} displays the nilpotent charge and the endpoint condition in the Hamiltonian setting.",
     ["bering1997geometry"],reconstruction=r"Nilpotence is a property of the odd derivation itself, not merely a vacuous consequence of the square of one Grassmann parameter being zero.",status=r"the algebraic identity is exact, with boundary conditions needed for the integrated action.")
item(478,"P000888","method","Field-dependent BRST change of variables and gauge independence",
     r"The last formula chooses \(\mu=i\int\mathcal P_a\,\delta\chi^a\,dt\) and concludes BRST invariance.",
     r"For an infinitesimal change of an admissible gauge fermion, a BRST change of path variables with a field-dependent odd parameter can compensate the change of gauge-fixing action through its Jacobian. This proves formal gauge independence only when the measure is BRST compatible, boundary terms vanish, the gauges are connected without a singularity, and no global orbit obstruction has been ignored.",
     r"\citet[\S~II, \S~V.2 and \S~V.3]{bering1997geometry} analyzes precisely this field-dependent path-space change and states explicitly that global Gribov problems, phase-space boundaries and existence of the path integral are excluded or assumed. Its arXiv preprint first circulated in September 1997; the mechanism itself is attributed there to the original BFV work.",
     ["bering1997geometry"],reconstruction=r"A field-dependent parameter does not generate an ordinary canonical transformation with unit Jacobian. The Jacobian, rather than constant-parameter invariance alone, is the crucial missing step.",match="partial",status=r"the qualified formal BFV theorem is established; the notes do not prove a global, nonperturbative gauge-independence theorem.")
