talk(480,"1979-12-10","I. Yu. Kobzarev","P000894-P000899","Confinement heuristics and the electroweak mass mechanism",
     r"The continuation first contrasts Coulomb fields with a proposed chromoelectric tube, with a short intervention attributed to Manin. It then derives the scalar Hessian, symmetry zero modes, gauge-boson masses, the Higgs-doublet breaking pattern, weak mixing, currents and fermion masses, ending with the quark mixing matrix. The confinement picture is distinguished from its still-open four-dimensional continuum formulation. The Higgs and weak-interaction material is principally exposition of older results, with explicit corrections to several notebook formulas.")
item(480,"P000894","claim","Coulomb interaction energy and divergent point-charge self-energies",
     r"The two-source field-line drawing accompanies \(\int E^2dV\sim e^2/r\).",
     r"For two static point charges in three spatial dimensions, subtracting the two separation-independent self-energies leaves an interaction \(V(r)=q_1q_2/(4\pi r)\) in rationalized units. The total \(\int|E|^2dV\) is divergent at the point charges unless they are regularized. Opposite charges have negative interaction energy even though the unsubtracted field-energy density is nonnegative.",
     r"The Abelian Coulomb behavior is contrasted with area-law confinement in \citet[\S~II]{wilson1974confinement}, published 15 October 1974. The electrostatic scaling itself is much older.",
     ["wilson1974confinement"],reconstruction=r"The source's schematic proportionality is read as the separation-dependent energy after self-energy subtraction, not the literal divergent total integral.",literal="correct with missing hypotheses",match="partial",status=r"the Coulomb comparison is established; the subtraction and charge-sign qualifications are essential.")
item(480,"P000894","conjecture","A flux tube of distance-independent transverse size",
     r"The page explicitly labels as a hypothesis that the tube cross-section or diameter does not depend on the separation of the sources.",
     r"A fixed intrinsic core profile in a classical flux-tube model and the quantum mean-square transverse width of a fluctuating string are different observables. In the long-string effective theory at zero temperature, the latter has leading behavior \(w^2(r/2)=(d-2)(2\pi\sigma)^{-1}\log(r/r_0)\). Thus a strictly fixed quantum width is incompatible with that effective description even when the leading energy is \(\sigma r\).",
     r"The later study \citet[eqs.~(1)--(3), with lattice comparison in Figs.~2--3]{gliozzipepewiese2010width} derives this broadening and tests it in \((2+1)\)-dimensional \(SU(2)\) lattice theory (preprint 26 February 2010; journal 2010). It cites the 1981 Lüscher--Münster--Weisz precursor. This is a developed correction of the naive picture, not a proof of the full four-dimensional continuum theory.",
     ["gliozzipepewiese2010width"],cls="B",literal="not determinable",match="partial",reconstruction=r"The notebook specifies neither a width observable nor a classical/quantum averaging prescription. The effective-theory statement is a precise later comparison; it is not silently substituted for an exact theorem about the source's unspecified diameter.",status=r"distance-independent width is not a universal confinement criterion. Quantum broadening and finite intrinsic thickness can coexist, and neither alone settles the continuum static-potential problem.",confidence="medium")
conf=item(480,"P000894","conjecture","Linear static energy and continuum confinement",
     r"Constant cross-section and energy density are used to infer a string with \(\nu=d\varepsilon/d\ell\sim E^2\sigma\), where the source's \(\sigma\) denotes cross-sectional area.",
     r"The elementary conditional inference is that constant energy per unit length gives \(V(r)=\tau r+O(1)\). A distinct sharp gauge-theory question is whether four-dimensional continuum pure \(SU(3)\) Yang--Mills theory at zero temperature has a positive asymptotic fundamental static string tension: \(\lim_{r\to\infty}V(r)/r=\tau>0\), where \(V(r)=-\lim_{T\to\infty}T^{-1}\log\langle W(r,T)\rangle\) after the appropriate static self-energy subtraction. Dynamical fundamental quarks are excluded from that asymptotic formulation.",
     r"\citet[\S~IV, contrasted with \S~VI]{wilson1974confinement} establishes the lattice strong-coupling mechanism, not the continuum limit. \citet[\S4, eq.~(4.4) and Figs.~2--3]{bulava2019string} demonstrates string breaking in lattice QCD with light and strange dynamical quarks (preprint 11 February 2019; journal 2019). \citet[\S5]{jaffewitten2000yangmills} separates confinement from the existence-and-mass-gap problem; the latter remains officially unsolved in \citet[problem status]{clay2026yangmills}.",
     ["wilson1974confinement","bulava2019string","jaffewitten2000yangmills","clay2026yangmills"],cls="C",reconstruction=r"The continuum, infinite-distance, zero-temperature and pure-gauge quantifiers are supplied to match the earlier problem 1975-377.10. The fixed-width hypothesis in item 1979-480.2 is stronger and is not part of that reused problem. The notation \(\tau\) avoids confusing tension with the source's area \(\sigma\).",literal="correct with missing hypotheses",match="partial",status=r"the conditional tube-energy calculation is elementary; a complete rigorous four-dimensional continuum construction proving this asymptotic potential was not found in the checked literature. Lattice evidence and effective strings do not complete that theorem, and string breaking invalidates the same asymptotic assertion with dynamical quarks.",problem="GP-1975-10",worth=r"A proof of a continuum positive string tension would be fundamental, but a bounded chapter calculation can only clarify the hypotheses and known controlled limits, not resolve confinement.",confidence="high")
problems.append(dict(id="GP-1975-10",title="Continuum confinement and the static potential",statement=r"In four-dimensional continuum pure non-Abelian Yang--Mills theory with external fundamental static sources, establish a well-defined subtracted static potential and a positive asymptotic string tension, \(V(r)/r\to\tau>0\) as \(r\to\infty\). The current occurrence specializes the gauge group to \(SU(3)\), at zero temperature and without dynamical fundamental quarks. This reuses the earlier pure-gauge static-potential question; it does not incorporate the stronger fixed-width hypothesis.",occurrences=["1975-377.10",conf],status=r"As of November 2026, strong-coupling lattice results, numerical evidence and effective-string calculations partly develop the problem; a complete continuum proof was not recovered. With light dynamical quarks, string breaking changes the asymptotic static-energy statement. The problem is distinct from, and stronger in a different direction than, simply stating a mass gap.",status_date="2026-11",refs=["wilson1974confinement","bulava2019string","jaffewitten2000yangmills","clay2026yangmills"]));save()
item(480,"P000894","remark","Manin's semiclassical qualification",
     r"A remark attributed to Manin refers to quantizing Yang--Mills in a semiclassical approximation and says that no exact model yet exists.",
     r"The recorded fragment gives no background solution, expansion parameter, observable or proposition about semiclassical asymptotics. It can qualify the preceding heuristic, but cannot be reconstructed as a definite semiclassical theorem or a separately quantified open problem.",
     r"The distinction between a formal or approximate gauge calculation and a construction of continuum quantum Yang--Mills theory is made explicit in \citet[\S\S2, 5--6]{jaffewitten2000yangmills}. This is context, not an exact publication match for Manin's unfinished sentence.",
     ["jaffewitten2000yangmills"],cls="F",literal="not determinable",match="thematic only",reconstruction=r"No instanton, soliton or vacuum saddle is supplied merely because the word semiclassical occurs.",status=r"the precise intended approximation remains unidentified; the broader construction problem is recorded separately.",confidence="low",speaker="Yu. I. Manin")
item(480,"P000894-P000895","construction","The product gauge connection and electroweak kinetic terms",
     r"The weak Lagrangian combines three \(SU(2)\) fields \(W^a\), an Abelian field \(B\), matter multiplets and a scalar multiplet; \(D_\mu=\partial_\mu+igt^aW^a_\mu+ig'yB_\mu\).",
     r"For \(SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y\), the connection has Lie-algebra components in \(\mathfrak{su}(2)\oplus\mathfrak u(1)\). Its adjoint representation is a triplet plus a trivial one-dimensional factor. The gauge kinetic terms are \(-\tfrac14W^a_{\mu\nu}W^{a\mu\nu}-\tfrac14B_{\mu\nu}B^{\mu\nu}\); the Abelian curvature has no commutator term. Each matter covariant derivative uses that matter field's own representation and hypercharge.",
     r"\citet[eqs.~(1)--(4)]{weinberg1967leptons} gives the older model, and \citet[\S10.1]{deblasdittmaierkogler2026electroweak} states the normalized modern Lagrangian.",
     ["weinberg1967leptons","deblasdittmaierkogler2026electroweak"],reconstruction=r"The notebook's alternation between \(A\), \(W\) and \(H\) for fields or curvatures is disambiguated. A coupling omitted from a curvature can be absorbed by rescaling its connection; the choice must be consistent with the kinetic term.",status=r"the product-connection construction is established; its three-plus-one adjoint decomposition is not the representation of every fermion or scalar.")
item(480,"P000895","definition","Scalar energy, stationary points and local vacua",
     r"The scalar energy is \(T_{00}=\tfrac12\dot\varphi^2+\tfrac12|\nabla\varphi|^2+P(\varphi)\), called positive, and any local minimum of \(P\) is called a vacuum.",
     r"With positive scalar kinetic form and a potential shifted so that its global minimum is zero, the classical energy density is nonnegative. In a trivial sector with compatible constant boundary data a constant global minimum minimizes the energy. A local minimum satisfies \(\nabla P=0\) and gives a candidate metastable state; stationary points alone need not be minima, and noncompact field space need not contain a global minimizer.",
     r"The earlier scalar symmetry-breaking examples are \citet[eqs.~(1)--(2)]{higgs1964broken} and \citet[eqs.~(3)--(5)]{weinberg1967leptons}.",
     ["higgs1964broken","weinberg1967leptons"],reconstruction=r"The energy shift, boundedness/existence assumptions and distinction between local and global minima are supplied. The word vacuum in the source is explicitly a local classical convention.",match="partial",status=r"the qualified energy argument is elementary; it does not establish a unique stable quantum vacuum.")
item(480,"P000895-P000896","theorem","Small oscillations and the scalar mass-squared Hessian",
     r"Linearization at \(\lambda\) gives \(\Box\varphi^\alpha+P_{,\alpha\beta}(\lambda)\varphi^\beta=0\), but the subsequent plane-wave equation inserts an additional factor \(1/2\).",
     r"For canonical kinetic term \(\tfrac12\partial_\mu\phi^\alpha\partial^\mu\phi^\alpha\) and \(\nabla P(\lambda)=0\), the mass-squared matrix is \(H_{\alpha\beta}=P_{,\alpha\beta}(\lambda)\). A plane wave obeys \(Hc=m^2c\), with no factor \(1/2\). The half in the Taylor expansion \(P(\lambda+\phi)=P(\lambda)+\tfrac12\phi^TH\phi+\cdots\) disappears when the equation is varied. A noncanonical kinetic metric would give a generalized eigenvalue problem instead.",
     r"The scalar and vector fluctuation masses are already derived in \citet[eqs.~(2a)--(4)]{higgs1964broken}; the Hessian statement here also follows directly from the notebook's previous equation.",
     ["higgs1964broken"],reconstruction=r"The factor is corrected against both the scan and the Euler--Lagrange equation, not silently changed in the transcription.",literal="false as written",match="partial",status=r"the corrected classical mass matrix is settled; the notebook's extra half is inconsistent with its canonical normalization.")
item(480,"P000896","theorem","Symmetry zero modes and the correct Goldstone count",
     r"Differentiating \(P(g\varphi)=P(\varphi)\) gives zero modes \(I^a\lambda\). The text initially counts the generators but then observes that some vectors can be dependent.",
     r"Let a compact Lie group \(G\) act orthogonally on a real scalar space, let \(P\) be invariant, and let \(\lambda\) be a stationary point. Differentiating the invariance identity gives \(H(T\lambda)=0\) for every \(T\in\mathfrak g\). Thus the Hessian kernel contains the tangent space of the orbit and has dimension at least \(r=\dim G-\dim G_\lambda\). Equality requires nondegeneracy transverse to the orbit; accidental flat directions can give additional zeros.",
     r"The gauge-model relation to the older Goldstone result is discussed in \citet[opening paragraphs and eqs.~(2a)--(2c)]{higgs1964broken}. The finite-dimensional Hessian identity is explicitly derived in this notebook; it does not by itself prove the full quantum Goldstone theorem.",
     ["higgs1964broken"],reconstruction=r"The rank of the orbit, rather than the dimension of the representation or all generators, is the exact symmetry count. Stationarity is used when the term containing \(\nabla P\) is dropped.",match="partial",status=r"the classical kernel statement is exact; the source's later dependence qualification repairs its initial overcount.")
item(480,"P000896-P000897","construction","Unitary gauge, vector masses and conserved physical degrees of freedom",
     r"A gauge is chosen to remove scalar components along Goldstone directions; the connection then acquires a quadratic mass term. The page counts \(2n\to3n\) vector polarizations and \(N\to N-n\) scalar components.",
     r"Locally near a nonzero vacuum on a fixed orbit-type stratum, choose a slice transverse to the \(r\)-dimensional gauge orbit. The scalar kinetic term produces the positive semidefinite Gram mass matrix \((M^2)_{ab}=g_ag_b\langle T_a\lambda,T_b\lambda\rangle\), of rank \(r\). In four spacetime dimensions, \(r\) vector fields have three physical polarizations and \(n-r\) remain massless with two; \(N-r\) scalar components remain. The total count is \(2n+N=3r+2(n-r)+(N-r)\).",
     r"\citet[eqs.~(2)--(4)]{higgs1964broken} gives the 1964 Abelian mechanism explicitly, including the new massive vector and remaining scalar; \citet[eqs.~(5)--(12)]{weinberg1967leptons} applies the partial breaking relevant here.",
     ["higgs1964broken","weinberg1967leptons"],reconstruction=r"The source's \(3n\) count assumes every generator is broken. Unitary gauge is local and can fail at zeros of the scalar field or across different orbit strata; the claimed choice at every point is not a global theorem.",match="partial",status=r"the local classical Higgs mechanism is established; it redistributes physical polarizations without increasing their total number.")
item(480,"P000896-P000897","question","Exercise: eliminate the Goldstone field in the planar scalar model",
     r"The explicit exercise asks to eliminate the Goldstone mode for \(P=\tfrac12(\varphi_1^2+\varphi_2^2-\eta^2)^2\), with \(G=O(2)\), and suggests shifting \(A\) by a derivative of an angle.",
     r"On a patch with \(|\varphi|>0\), write \(\varphi=(\eta+h)(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)\). For the connected \(SO(2)\) gauge action and convention \(D=\partial+gAJ\), set \(B_\mu=A_\mu+g^{-1}\partial_\mu\theta\). Then \(\tfrac12|D\varphi|^2=\tfrac12(\partial h)^2+\tfrac12g^2(\eta+h)^2B_\mu B^\mu\). The angular field disappears locally; the quadratic masses are \(m_B^2=g^2\eta^2\) and \(m_h^2=4\eta^2\) for the notebook's potential normalization.",
     r"This is the elementary local calculation underlying \citet[eqs.~(1)--(4)]{higgs1964broken}, already published in 1964. The exercise is answered here as an annotation; it is not an unresolved problem or a new research result.",
     ["higgs1964broken"],reconstruction=r"The coupling convention, connected subgroup and nonvanishing-field patch are supplied. The disconnected reflection in \(O(2)\) supplies no additional infinitesimal Goldstone generator.",status=r"the exercise has the displayed standard solution; a global angle is not asserted in sectors with zeros or winding.")
item(480,"P000897-P000899","construction","The Higgs doublet and the unbroken electromagnetic subgroup",
     r"The scalar is a two-component complex spinor with four real components. A gauge rotation puts it in \((0,\varphi_2)\), and one combination of \(t_3\) and hypercharge annihilates that vector. Three Goldstones are said to be eaten.",
     r"Take a complex \(SU(2)\) doublet \(\Phi\) with \(Y=1/2\), and \(\langle\Phi\rangle=(0,v/\sqrt2)\). Then \(Q=T_3+Y\) annihilates the vacuum, leaving \(U(1)_{\rm em}\) unbroken. The orbit has dimension three: three of the four real scalar components supply longitudinal vector polarizations and one physical radial scalar remains. Spinor here describes the weak fundamental representation; the Higgs field is a Lorentz scalar.",
     r"\citet[eqs.~(3)--(12)]{weinberg1967leptons} is the direct pre-1979 model; \citet[\S10.1]{deblasdittmaierkogler2026electroweak} fixes the current doublet normalization.",
     ["weinberg1967leptons","deblasdittmaierkogler2026electroweak"],reconstruction=r"The notebook's hypercharge symbol is rescaled and sign-changed on P000897; choosing \(Q=T_3+Y\) avoids treating that convention as a physical discrepancy. Gauging away three components is local near the chosen nonzero vacuum.",status=r"this symmetry-breaking pattern and particle count are established; the remaining scalar's mass is a potential parameter, not fixed by the symmetry alone.")
item(480,"P000898","theorem","Diagonalizing the weak neutral mass matrix",
     r"The displayed four-by-four matrix has charged entries \(a^2,a^2\) and neutral block \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a^2&ab\\ab&b^2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\), with \(a\) proportional to \(g\) and \(b\) to \(g'\). It yields \(\tan\theta_w=g'/g\) and \(m_Z=m_W/\cos\theta_w\).",
     r"The neutral block has determinant zero, eigenvalues \(0,a^2+b^2\), and orthogonal eigenvectors \((-b,a)\) and \((a,b)\). With the standard doublet normalization, \(m_W^2=g^2v^2/4\), \(m_Z^2=(g^2+g'^2)v^2/4\), and \(m_\gamma=0\), so \(m_W=m_Z\cos\theta_w\) at tree level. Changing the sign of the Abelian connection changes the off-diagonal sign but not the spectrum.",
     r"\citet[eqs.~(5)--(12)]{weinberg1967leptons} derives the direct earlier result. Radiative corrections and the distinction between mixing-angle schemes and measured masses are explicit in \citet[\S\S10.1--10.2]{deblasdittmaierkogler2026electroweak}.",
     ["weinberg1967leptons","deblasdittmaierkogler2026electroweak"],reconstruction=r"The vertical bars in equation (33) are read as delimiting the mass matrix, not merely as its determinant. The coupling relation requires the conventional hypercharge normalization and common vacuum scale.",status=r"the tree-level eigenvalue identity is exact; physical precision predictions include radiative corrections.")
item(480,"P000898","construction","Charged, electromagnetic and neutral currents",
     r"Equation (37) lists \(J^\pm,J^{\rm el},J^0\). Its neutral term has a prefactor read as \(q/\cos\theta_w\) and a single \(\sin\theta_w\) where the standard expression requires a square.",
     r"For fixed generator and field conventions, \[\mathcal L_{\rm int}=-\frac{g}{\sqrt2}(J^-_\mu W^{+\mu}+J^+_\mu W^{-\mu})-eJ^{\rm em}_\mu A^\mu-\frac{g}{\cos\theta_w}(J^3_\mu-\sin^2\theta_w J^{\rm em}_\mu)Z^\mu,\quad e=g\sin\theta_w.\] Here \(J^3\) contains left-handed isospin and \(J^{\rm em}\) contains both chiralities with their electric charges. Vertex factors follow by differentiating the interaction action; probabilities further require amplitudes, sums over states and phase space.",
     r"\citet[eqs.~(13)--(14)]{weinberg1967leptons} gives the earlier current structure; \citet[\S10.1]{deblasdittmaierkogler2026electroweak} provides the modern normalization and chiral couplings.",
     ["weinberg1967leptons","deblasdittmaierkogler2026electroweak"],reconstruction=r"The meanings of \(J^+\) and \(J^-\) are fixed with the chosen conjugate W fields. The neutral coupling and squared sine are emendations of the source; a charge operator cannot replace the universal weak prefactor.",literal="false as written",match="partial",status=r"the corrected electroweak current formula is established; the original two coefficient errors would change neutral-current amplitudes.")
item(480,"P000898-P000899","construction","Electron mass from a gauge-invariant Yukawa coupling",
     r"The left lepton doublet and right electron are paired with the Higgs vacuum vector; after choosing \((0,\lambda)\) the term is \(f\lambda\bar e_Re_L+\mathrm{h.c.}\).",
     r"With \(L_L\sim(\mathbf2,-1/2)\), \(e_R\sim(\mathbf1,-1)\), and \(\Phi\sim(\mathbf2,1/2)\), the invariant interaction is \(-y_e\bar L_L\Phi e_R+\mathrm{h.c.}\). At \(\Phi=(0,(v+h)/\sqrt2)\), it gives \(m_e=|y_e|v/\sqrt2\) after rephasing and the associated electron--Higgs coupling. The minimal field content leaves neutrinos massless; that is a limitation of this model, not an established property of actual neutrinos.",
     r"\citet[eqs.~(1), (3)--(5)]{weinberg1967leptons} gives the earlier electron Yukawa term. The modern distinction between the minimal model and neutrino mass extensions is \citet[\S\S14.1--14.2]{gonzalezgarciawendell2026neutrinos}.",
     ["weinberg1967leptons","gonzalezgarciawendell2026neutrinos"],reconstruction=r"The scalar or conjugate scalar in the invariant contraction depends on whether the term is written with \(\bar L_L\) or \(\bar e_R\); the precise invariant expression fixes the source's suppressed conjugation.",status=r"the Yukawa mechanism is established, while neutrino masses and mixing require an extension of the massless minimal field content.")
item(480,"P000899","question","Quark Lagrangian and a unitary mixing matrix",
     r"The final explicit question asks how to write the quark Lagrangian. Left-handed pairs use \((d',s',b')^T=V(d,s,b)^T\), and \(V\) is labeled orthogonal; right-handed quarks are singlets.",
     r"For each generation, \(Q_L\sim(\mathbf3,\mathbf2,1/6)\), \(u_R\sim(\mathbf3,\mathbf1,2/3)\), \(d_R\sim(\mathbf3,\mathbf1,-1/3)\). Add gauge kinetic couplings and \(-\bar Q_LY_d\Phi d_R-\bar Q_LY_u\widetilde\Phi u_R+\mathrm{h.c.}\), where \(\widetilde\Phi=i\sigma_2\Phi^*\). Biunitary diagonalization of \(Y_u,Y_d\) gives \(V=U_{uL}^\dagger U_{dL}\) in charged currents. In the three-generation minimal model \(V\) is unitary and has three physical angles and one CP-violating phase; it is real orthogonal only after imposing a CP-conserving restriction.",
     r"\citet[pp.~654--657]{kobayashimaskawa1973cp} introduced the six-quark CP-violating structure before this talk. \citet[\S12.1, eqs.~(12.1)--(12.3)]{ceccucciligetisakai2026ckm} gives the explicit Yukawa and unitary-mixing construction.",
     ["kobayashimaskawa1973cp","ceccucciligetisakai2026ckm"],reconstruction=r"The right-handed singlets refer only to weak \(SU(2)\); they still carry colour and hypercharge. The symbol resembling a superscript 1 on the down-type fields is interpreted through the clearly written primed mixing relation, not as a new generation label.",literal="false as written",match="partial",status=r"the question has the standard displayed construction. The unrestricted orthogonality claim omits the physical CP phase and is not an open problem.")
